# The places Polish speaks at that Italian does not, written for Polish rather
# than lifted for Italian. A locus rule says where the formants go; the rule
# for a phoneme sets its source and then calls one of these.
#
# The numbers are a starting point and the ear settles them. What they are
# read off is Italian's own: its palatal locus is f2 1800 and f3 2400, its
# dentals 1700 and 2600, and a labial's low second formant 850 -- so the
# language's own numbers say what scale these are on.

# Polish sz, ż, cz and dż are retroflex, articulated further back than the
# palato-alveolars Italian spells with sc and gi. The signature of a retroflex
# is a low third formant, so f3 sits below the soft series' and f2 below it
# too. Nothing here touches the first formant: the rule for the phoneme sets
# that before it asks for a place.
#
# The numbers are the ear's, not a textbook's. Three settings were built and
# listened to on the same five words -- 1700 and 2200, 1600 and 1900, 1800 and
# 2500 -- and the last was the one that sounded like Polish. Which says the
# first two were too far back for this synthesiser rather than too far back for
# the language: Italian's own palatal locus is 1800 and 2400, and what came out
# best is a hair above it.
rule pol_retroflex_Fv takes 1 from is_val.obj
  call ZZfence_null
  set f2_in to 1800
  set f2_out to 1800
  set f3_in to 2500
  set f3_out to 2500
  match
end

# And the soft series -- ś, ź, ć, dź -- which is further forward than either,
# with a third formant higher than the palatal's rather than lower. These
# numbers went to the ear the same way the hard pair's did: 2000 and 2700, 2100
# and 3000, 2200 and 3200, on the same five words with the hard pair held
# still, and the highest of the three was the one that sounded like Polish. It
# is seven hundred hertz clear of the hard series, which is what keeps kasza
# and Kasia apart. This is
# what Italian's own S and Z ask for now: their record says palatal and that is
# what the soft series is, so the two of them serve it as they stand.
# pol_sibilant_Fv below is what chooses between the two places.
rule pol_alveolopalatal_Fv takes 1 from is_val.obj
  call ZZfence_null
  set f2_in to 2200
  set f2_out to 2200
  set f3_in to 3200
  set f3_out to 3200
  match
end

# Which of the two sibilant places the phone about to be spoken wants.
#
# Polish has two series where Italian has one. The hard one -- sz, ż, cz, dż --
# is retroflex, the tongue curled back, and the soft one -- ś, ź, ć, dź -- is
# palatal, the tongue flat against the palate. They are different letters and
# different words: kasza is not Kasia.
#
# Italian's S and Z are palatal and IBM's own table says so, `place_of_artic
# pal', so those two serve the soft series as they stand. The hard series has
# to be two more phonemes and there is nowhere to put a new one: a phoneme
# code is tested by name in some five hundred places across the module -- every
# locus rule asks what its neighbours are, every vowel asks what consonant
# follows it, the durations and the syllabifier ask too -- and a code that has
# never existed is invisible to all of them. So the hard series took over the
# two of Italian's that no Polish word can reach: L, its palatal l from gli,
# and R, its strong trill. Their records now say fricative and retroflex, and
# the value chain hands them to Italian's own fricative rules.
#
# Which leaves one thing for a rule to decide, and this is it. ital_ph_S and
# ital_ph_Z ask for a place; the place depends on which of the two phones is
# being spoken, and the phone is the one after the position the language keeps
# in the variable this scan is loaded from.
rule pol_sibilant_Fv takes 1 from is_val.obj
  call ZZfence_null
  call ZZlpta_load_vvg__setscan_0103r__2
  if answer is 0
    call ZZtest_string_s_2_1_ZZstring113        # the hard sz
    if answer is 0
      call pol_retroflex_Fv
      match
    end
  end
  call ZZlpta_load_vvg__setscan_0103r__2
  if answer is 0
    call ZZtest_string_s_2_1_ZZstring103        # and the hard ż
    if answer is 0
      call pol_retroflex_Fv
      match
    end
  end
  # /x/ goes through the same fricative rule and wants the velar place, which
  # is Italian's own -- it is where k and g are spoken.
  call ZZlpta_load_vvg__setscan_0103r__2
  if answer is 0
    call pol_test_x
    if answer is 0
      call ital_velar_Fv
      match
    end
  end
  call pol_alveolopalatal_Fv
  match
end

# Whether the phone about to be spoken is Polish's /x/, the sound ch and h
# stand for. It has a code of its own -- there was nothing left to retake --
# and Italian's voiceless fricative rule speaks it, because that is what it is:
# the same manner as its sz and s, at a different place.
rule pol_test_x takes 1 from is_val.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 2 1 sym pol_ph_x
end

# Polish /x/ is a velar fricative: its noise is low, around the second
# formant, and weak, where Italian's S rule, which spoke it, makes the high
# hiss of a sibilant -- ch came out as sz. That rule sets its noise and hands
# it to the synthesiser in modulate_noise before it returns, so a rule that
# called it and set the noise afterwards would be too late. This is its steps
# with /x/'s numbers: the same setting at 2918 and 3014 that it makes, the
# velar locus, the noise, and modulate_noise. The doubled consonant it asks
# about before modulating is not a thing Polish ch does.
#
# The noise is the parallel branch do_fric_amps and modulate_noise read: the
# frication at 2754, and A2, A3, A4, A5, A6 and the bypass at 2770 to 2790.
# The numbers are the ear's.
rule pol_ph_x takes 1 from is_val.obj
  afresh
  variable g2918 half 2918
  variable g3014 half 3014
  variable noise half 2754
  variable a2 half 2770
  variable a3 half 2774
  variable a4 half 2778
  variable a5 half 2782
  variable a6 half 2786
  variable bypass half 2790
  call ZZfence_null
  set g2918 to 300
  call ital_velar_Fv
  set g3014 to 4
  set noise to 60
  set a2 to 76
  set a3 to 62
  set a4 to 0
  set a5 to 0
  set a6 to 0
  set bypass to 50
  call modulate_noise
  match
end

# Whether the phone after this one is in the same word. Italian times and
# shapes an n as its velar nasal whenever a velar follows, in ital_ph_n and
# ital_ph_n_dur alike, and asks nothing about where the word ends, so the n
# of ten gwóźdź was the quieter velar murmur of bank and ten sounded clipped.
# The fence on the word statement is what tells, since the scan cannot be
# stepped out of a word through it. Both rules have the fence down and the
# scan set after the phone when they ask, and that is how it is left.
rule pol_next_in_word takes 1 from is_val.obj
  bare
  afresh
  variable after word 840
  call fence 1 sym pol_fence_word
  call lpta_loadp addr after
  call setscan_nof_r 2
  if answer is 0
    call advance_tok
  end
  if answer is not 0
    call ZZfence_null
    call lpta_loadp addr after
    call setscan_r 2
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZfence_null
  call lpta_loadp addr after
  call setscan_r 2
  answer 0
end
